WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:11.040
I’ve come face to face with a God. He lives&nbsp;
in the sky, and he’s a giant flying whale.&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.320
If you’ve played the game Skyward Sword,&nbsp;
you know what I’m talking about. Levias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.280
spirit of the skies, is a pretty awe-inspiring&nbsp;
sight for a Wii game. When I was little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:28.080
I spent hours circling around this deity, at&nbsp;
times coming so close to divinity I felt I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:34.160
could reach out and touch it. So, you can imagine&nbsp;
my excitement when I learned that Breath of Wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.387
a game set thousands of years&nbsp;
later, also includes Levias.&nbsp;

00:00:40.387 --> 00:00:49.000
He’s dead.&nbsp;

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
There are many stories in which divine beings&nbsp;
come to an end. But what happens after a god&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.800
dies? Would they… decompose, their godly forms&nbsp;
rotting away to nothing? Or would they linger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.280
their figures forever changing the surrounding&nbsp;
environment, or even becoming an environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:25.960
themselves? Would they ever really die?
(Waves Crashing) The short film The Drowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:34.000
Giant is about — well, a drowned giant, obviously.&nbsp;
But that obviousness is the point. Washed up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.280
an otherwise ordinary beach near a small town,&nbsp;
the giant’s corpse is a blunt truth — something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:46.720
the narrator and other locals cannot ignore or&nbsp;
rationalize. Without any context for where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:54.720
body came from, it’s at first treated as a deity,&nbsp;
the crowd reverential and apprehensive. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:02.560
something changes. This colossus, be it god&nbsp;
or monster, begins to rot. And just like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.760
the spell is broken. The townspeople swarm the&nbsp;
fallen titan, clamoring over its immense form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:14.440
as if trying to reestablish their own importance&nbsp;
relative to the superbeing. Over the following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:19.680
days, animals descend upon the carcass as&nbsp;
well, hastening the disfigurement of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.320
remains. Though disturbed by the general lack of&nbsp;
respect, the narrator cannot, in the end, resist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:33.880
the urge to climb upon the corpse himself. The&nbsp;
fact that the Giant’s decay, its decomposition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:39.640
is what strips away its godhood in the eyes of&nbsp;
the masses speaks to the inherent paradox of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:46.760
dead deity. In most classical mythologies, dying&nbsp;
is the pastime of mortals — what makes a god a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:53.600
god is their immortality, their permeance. The&nbsp;
narrator has the sense the giant will reawaken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.280
that something so powerful in life must still&nbsp;
hold power after death — but the body remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.960
motionless. Soon, people see it as just another&nbsp;
piece of trash, an unsightly obstruction that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:11.760
should be disposed of. They eventually decide&nbsp;
to cut the body into sections and haul it away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:19.000
piece by piece. The Drowned Giant is ultimately&nbsp;
a melancholy examination of mortality, of how any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:26.840
being can be disrespected and forgotten, no matter&nbsp;
how large they loomed in life. It is one thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:32.600
gaze upon the corpse of a god you did not know&nbsp;
in life. It is another thing entirely to know a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:40.000
god before it falls, and know a god after. In the&nbsp;
Xenoblade games, godly beings called Titans are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.320
numerous that it’s actually hard to keep track of&nbsp;
them all. That is, except for in the third game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:50.320
which makes things simpler by turning all&nbsp;
Titans into lifeless husks that now make up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.200
landscape. The reasons for this are — too much to&nbsp;
get into, trust me. But the tragedy here is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.520
straightforward. Here are the shells of beings&nbsp;
that you know if you’ve played the other titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.120
in the series. The game doesn’t draw attention&nbsp;
to these remnants either, which is notable, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:14.560
Xenoblade is, on balance, a pretty loud franchise.&nbsp;
Instead, the remains quietly fade into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:21.880
background. Most characters don’t even remember&nbsp;
what they were. I think what saddened me the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:27.360
about Levias’ corpse in Breath of the Wild — you&nbsp;
know, aside from my celestial whale friend being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.240
pile of bones — is the fact that no one seems to&nbsp;
remember this deity. There are some researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:39.200
who debate his identity, but they assume he was&nbsp;
just a large extinct animal, not the god I knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.720
him to be. But I really can’t judge, because there&nbsp;
are actually other giant whale skeletons scattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:51.680
throughout this game. When I first saw these, I&nbsp;
didn’t know what to make of them. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.680
they’re actually the remains of other whale gods&nbsp;
from throughout the series, lost deities that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.480
didn’t even know about. For beings that were once&nbsp;
worshiped, it’s hard to imagine a more tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:12.920
fate. These gods haven’t just died… they’ve been&nbsp;
forgotten. (Chanting) One isn’t likely to forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:19.600
the deities of the God of War saga. In the current&nbsp;
series based on Norse mythology, clashes between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:26.520
gods and other divine beings regularly reshape&nbsp;
the landscape. The sheer scale of godly power that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:33.680
these games capture is just astounding, creating&nbsp;
countless unforgettable moments. It’s curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.720
then, that the experience that stuck in my mind&nbsp;
the most wasn’t meeting a live god, but a dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:46.160
one. Midway through the first game in the Norse&nbsp;
saga, you arrive upon the mountainous corpse of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:52.400
Thamur. Though technically a giant rather than a&nbsp;
god, in-game lore suggests Thamur had the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:59.720
to challenge Thor himself, a power so great,&nbsp;
it continues to shape the world in death. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:06.160
decaying, Thamur’s body emits a maelstrom of&nbsp;
cold that has frozen the surrounding ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.360
an effect you can see from miles away. His sheer&nbsp;
mass also flattened an entire village when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:18.240
first fell. This idea is also present in the Greek&nbsp;
God of War titles, where the death of a pantheon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.480
member had an immediate and apocalyptic effect on&nbsp;
whatever domain they had authority over. Unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:31.440
the fallen gods discussed so far, Thamur still&nbsp;
holds influence over the environment in his icy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.960
cadaverous grip. You don’t just observe the&nbsp;
power of Thamur’s corpse from a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:43.600
though — you have to climb it. And the act of&nbsp;
scrabbling up the side of this colossus, like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.160
was one of the townsfolk from The Drowned Giant,&nbsp;
conveyed to me the scale of a god’s power better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:55.200
than any of the series’ most gonzo action beats.&nbsp;
When I recently replayed Breath of the Wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.080
I tried clambering up the leviathan skeletons,&nbsp;
an experience that reminded me how small I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:10.880
in comparison. And that even in death, these&nbsp;
gods, too, still hold power. At a glance, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:17.560
doesn’t seem like there’s a dead god in the game&nbsp;
Jusant. What there is, dominating every frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:24.160
filling every inch of your thoughts — is the&nbsp;
mountain. And your job, your singular goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:30.760
is to reach the summit. As in God of War, the&nbsp;
very act of climbing in Jusant feels like a form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:37.360
of worship. Every handhold is a prayer, every&nbsp;
completed route a hymn to the mountain’s power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:43.120
Jusant also requires you to make these leaps&nbsp;
of — well, faith, for lack of a better word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.600
where you’re suspended on nothing but a rope and&nbsp;
the trust that the mountain will hold you. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:54.000
in these moments that you can get the closest to&nbsp;
fathoming the true size of the mountain’s body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:00.800
to comprehending the extent of its power. You&nbsp;
encounter the remains of multiple civilizations as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:07.120
you make your way up this peak, cultures that rose&nbsp;
and fell entirely within its shadow. It is clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.520
in the art these societies have left behind that&nbsp;
this mountain was something they worshiped. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:21.920
the people who lived upon its form, this mountain&nbsp;
was — is — a kind of god. And this god has died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:28.160
There is a sense that the present-day peak is a&nbsp;
husk — a dried out skeleton of its former self.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.520
The wreckage of boats, fishing equipment, and&nbsp;
withered coral that scar the landscape indicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:40.520
a vast ocean once surrounded this region. The&nbsp;
game’s very title, Jusant, a French maritime term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:46.880
for a period of low tide, supports this narrative.&nbsp;
Without getting into spoilers, there’s evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:54.240
this drought came from human activity throwing the&nbsp;
ecosystem out of balance. This mountain was a god,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:02.960
and we have killed it. There is a polluted,&nbsp;
corpse-strewn deity on the face of the Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:09.240
Its name is Mount Everest. Actually, its name&nbsp;
is Chomolungma in native Tibetan, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:15.400
“Goddess Mother of the World.” The name Everest&nbsp;
comes from Welsh cartographer George Everest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:22.000
a man who famously disliked the idea of a deified&nbsp;
peak being named after an outsider. But the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:29.880
stuck with the English-speaking world — and so too&nbsp;
did an obsession with scaling this icy God. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:35.920
hundreds of people climb to the summit every year,&nbsp;
many of whom are wealthy, inexperienced outsiders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:44.520
following well-traveled routes. Yet Chomolungma or&nbsp;
Everest still holds frigid, unpredictable power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:51.440
In his book Into Thin Air, author Jon Krakauer&nbsp;
writes a firsthand account of the 1996 Everest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:58.160
disaster, a blizzard in which eight climbers&nbsp;
lost their lives. This event was a tragedy… but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:04.800
also not uncommon. Since the 1920s, over three&nbsp;
hundred people have died trying to reach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:10.120
peak — a staggering one third of whom have been&nbsp;
guides from the Sherpa community, an ethnic group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:15.560
indigenous to the Himalayan subregion. Outsiders&nbsp;
have long relied on Sherpa guides to make it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:20.680
the top of the mountain. Edmund Hillary, often&nbsp;
called the first person to summit Everest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:27.160
did so alongside Sherpa mountaineer Tenzing&nbsp;
Norgay. Guides face significant exploitation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:33.720
inconsistent safety measures, often carrying much&nbsp;
of a client’s gear. Between oxygen tanks, tents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:39.880
and food containers, literal tons of garbage have&nbsp;
been left on the slopes. In their haste to ascend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:46.840
a god, tourists have left her body disfigured&nbsp;
and the community disrupted. In the epilogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:52.800
of “Into Thin Air,” Krakauer relays the account of&nbsp;
a sherpa orphan lamenting the state of the sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:59.600
mountain, stating: “Outsiders find their way into&nbsp;
the sanctuary and violate every limb of her body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.320
by standing on top of her, crowing in victory,&nbsp;
and dirtying and polluting her…” For many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:14.640
climbing Everest is clearly not an act of worship.&nbsp;
There’s clearly something wrong with the holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.360
mountain in Horizon: Zero Dawn. I could tell there&nbsp;
was something wrong from the start of the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:26.080
when I looked up and saw these eldritch metal&nbsp;
tentacles, spilling out of the mountain like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.520
mechanical intestines. A post-apocalyptic&nbsp;
title, Horizon is full of the wreckage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:37.920
civilization — the skeletons of buildings and cars&nbsp;
and lampposts—and for most part these ruins feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:44.080
benign. The only thing menacing about them is&nbsp;
what they imply: that some terrible fate befell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:52.240
humanity. But the ruins on the holy mountain don’t&nbsp;
feel benign. They feel like a disease. It isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:58.560
the exterior of the mountain that the survivors&nbsp;
in Horizon worship, however. It’s the interior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:04.560
a chamber containing a god called All-Mother.&nbsp;
To the viewer, it quickly becomes clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:10.440
this god is a fragment of ancient technology,&nbsp;
one the survivors revere for saving them from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:16.760
an apocalyptic danger. The tentacled corpse smote&nbsp;
upon the mountainside also has a name — although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:22.680
it isn’t called a god. It’s called the&nbsp;
Metal Devil. And once you hear that name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:30.120
the story of what happened to the world starts to&nbsp;
fall into place. In fiction, the corpse of a devil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:35.840
has a very different presence than the corpse of&nbsp;
a god. In the Studio Ghibli film “Naussica of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:41.880
Valley of the Wind,” civilization was wiped out by&nbsp;
biomechanical weapons of mass destruction called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:48.000
God Warriors — although most survivors now see&nbsp;
these creations as demons. But in both “Naussica”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:54.320
and Horizon, there are characters who seek power&nbsp;
in these accursed remains, attempting to wield the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:00.600
rotting, long-fallen devils against their enemies.&nbsp;
We’re getting into spoiler territory here, but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:06.680
each narrative, attempts to wield these demonic&nbsp;
weapons end in further apocalyptic disaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:13.000
the characters inevitably losing control, undone&nbsp;
by the same human arrogance that had previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:18.920
destroyed the world. It’s no accident that both&nbsp;
stories contrast these remains with nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:25.200
framing them as the consequence of disrespecting&nbsp;
the natural order. Ultimately, these demonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:33.880
corpses feel like humanity’s punishment for&nbsp;
trying to play God. Human hubris can become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:41.720
its own fallen god. There’s a decomposing colossus&nbsp;
punishing the characters in the 2014 Russian movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:47.760
Leviathan. And it’s not the giant, mysterious&nbsp;
whale skeleton you see throughout the runtime — or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:54.840
at least, not entirely. The true deity putrefying&nbsp;
in the background of Leviathan is, instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.840 --> 00:14:01.240
a societal one. All of civilization that we see&nbsp;
throughout the film feels like it’s rotting from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:06.440
the inside out, infested with governmental&nbsp;
corruption. The film’s title appears in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:12.360
reference to the Hobbesian Leviathan, or at least&nbsp;
an idealized version of it — the deity of society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:18.880
one buys into for a nation to function. In the&nbsp;
film, this deity is rapidly decaying. Wooden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:25.440
ships like ribcages collapse upon the shore. A&nbsp;
fish processing plant calls to mind a slowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:31.280
circulatory system, all the workers inside&nbsp;
the final blood cells dragging this organism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:36.800
along before the inevitable. A condemned&nbsp;
house is picked apart like a beached whale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:42.200
first by stragglers and then by the apex&nbsp;
predator of a demolition machine. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:48.040
not technically set in the post apocalypse,&nbsp;
this world seems to have died a long time ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:55.760
and is only now beginning to stink. I think part&nbsp;
of why the dead Leviathans in Breath of the Wild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:01.440
struck such a chord with me… has less to do with&nbsp;
the state of the remains, and more to do with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:06.880
state of the world around them. Though it doesn’t&nbsp;
always look it, Breath of the Wild is a true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:14.160
post-apocalyptic narrative, set after a calamity&nbsp;
destroyed much of civilization. This devastation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:21.360
is embodied in the appearance of another deity in&nbsp;
the series. In Skyward Sword, the Goddess Hylia is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:28.280
represented by a shining statue — fitting for an&nbsp;
era of relative prosperity. In Breath of the Wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:34.920
the statue of Hylia is overgrown, forgotten&nbsp;
in a half-demolished cathedral abandoned years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:41.440
ago. It’s a testament to how much of the world has&nbsp;
been lost post-calamity. I think that’s what I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:48.200
really mourning. Not just Levias, but the larger,&nbsp;
abstract ‘Leviathan’ of a functioning society. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:55.160
a Hobbesian one, but the kind of community that&nbsp;
wouldn’t have forgotten this deity so easily. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.160 --> 00:16:02.080
must a dead god represent an end, or could it also&nbsp;
represent a new beginning? Though less common,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:08.040
there are stories where a god’s death brings new&nbsp;
life, their remains serving as a foundation upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:13.640
which something new is built. The Marvel Comics&nbsp;
city of Knowhere is built in the skull of a fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:19.920
celestial — basically that universe’s equivalent&nbsp;
of a god. In the animated series The Owl House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:26.640
an entire civilization thrives on the bones of a&nbsp;
still-rotting deity. I’ve said the word ‘rot’ a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:32.960
bunch in this video, but in nature, rotting isn’t&nbsp;
something inherently negative. The decomposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:39.320
remains of a leviathan like a whale can feed&nbsp;
other lifeforms for years — even decades. Such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:44.520
events are called whalefalls, and while I talk&nbsp;
more about them in my video on Dead Worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:49.320
it’s worth reiterating just how much life&nbsp;
these sea gods bring to the ocean floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:58.520
their skeletons becoming miniature ecosystems. Has&nbsp;
such a lifeform even died? But there is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:05.440
kind of godly skeleton that produces another kind&nbsp;
of rot. The eeriest region of the game Subnautica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:12.000
is the Lost River, a noxious environment flooded&nbsp;
with toxic brine. And at the center of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:18.280
fallout are the bones of the Gargantuan Leviathan,&nbsp;
a creature so much larger than the other in-game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:24.640
lifeforms it borders on the divine. There’s a&nbsp;
sense that the Leviathan’s decomposition is what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:31.040
creating this poisonous brine, that it’s power&nbsp;
still haunts the environment after death. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:37.040
even here, there are creatures that have adapted&nbsp;
to this briny wasteland. In fact, some species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:43.400
seem to flourish down here, their entire lifecycle&nbsp;
tied to these pestilent remains. Life upon such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:52.480
a polluted, adversarial Leviathan is a struggle,&nbsp;
it’s unpleasant — but it’s still a life. On Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:58.520
polluted holy sites are rarely abandoned by the&nbsp;
people who worship them. There are no statistics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:03.000
on how many sacred bodies of water or mountains&nbsp;
have been contaminated over the years — but a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:08.680
quick search shows you the numbers are in the&nbsp;
hundreds, if not the thousands. Many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:14.400
sites are still visited regularly by individuals&nbsp;
who must risk their own health to continue their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:20.320
way of life. And most of these sacred landmarks&nbsp;
are far from beyond saving. Even Everest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:26.320
despite the severity of its trash accumulation,&nbsp;
could be made into a healthy region once more. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:34.640
it would require effort, a change in the way these&nbsp;
places are unprotected. It would require struggle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:40.240
Hyper Light Drifter is a game about fighting&nbsp;
for your life, no matter the conditions or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:46.880
fallen titans before you. At least, I think that’s&nbsp;
what it’s about. See, Hyper Light Drifter doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:52.480
contain a single line of dialogue, communicating&nbsp;
its themes entirely through visual and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:58.000
environmental storytelling. And the visuals that&nbsp;
leave the biggest impact are easily the remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:05.160
of the Titans. These immense beings lie scattered&nbsp;
throughout the land, and their presence is —well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:11.400
it’s disheartening to say the least. Because&nbsp;
there is a rot, a sickness, slowly overtaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:16.600
the character you play as, seemingly related to&nbsp;
whatever wiped out the Titans. Though there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:23.400
evidence the Titans weren’t the nicest creatures,&nbsp;
if these beings, these gods, have all perished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:31.080
what chance do you have? The theme of Hyper Light&nbsp;
Drifter is rooted in personal experience. Lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:37.120
designer Alx Preston was born with a deteriorating&nbsp;
heart disease, which he cites as an inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:42.960
for the game’s presentation. You can also see this&nbsp;
in the title of the game’s studio, Heart Machine…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:48.840
and in the giant, literal Titan’s heart you&nbsp;
can find, that, despite being robbed of a body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:54.680
continues to beat. Like the drowned giant, one&nbsp;
of the Titans has been disrespectfully cut into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.680 --> 00:20:01.160
pieces, and yet its fractured parts continue to&nbsp;
function. Alx Preston — who is still making games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:06.960
by the way — knew Hyper Light Drifter might be&nbsp;
his last ever project, and yet he pushed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:14.360
and created something that will last into&nbsp;
the future. At the end of The Drowned Giant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:19.320
the narrator is surprised to find some of the&nbsp;
being’s bones have become part of the town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:25.320
its scattered parts becoming structures and&nbsp;
supports for houses. Though the Giant is gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:31.480
its influence is not. It reminds me of a story&nbsp;
in Norse mythology. Though only briefly mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:36.640
in God of War, in the original Norse myth&nbsp;
there’s another giant-turned-divine-corpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:43.080
called Ymir. Following his death, Ymir’s body&nbsp;
becomes the foundation for the entire world:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:48.960
“Out of Ymir’s flesh was fashioned the earth, and&nbsp;
the mountains were made of his bones; The sky from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:55.680
the cold frost giant’s skull, and the ocean out of&nbsp;
his blood.” Something can still be made from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:03.040
body of a fallen leviathan, even if it isn’t the&nbsp;
same as what it once was. Maybe it’s wrong of me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:09.160
to wish Levias had stayed the same. I don’t know&nbsp;
what life he might have nurtured, what ecosystems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:16.600
fed on his remains. Levias still exists, both in&nbsp;
my memories (and on my Wii if I turn it back on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:22.600
because he’s from a video game but, you know,&nbsp;
metaphor.) The Leviathan of civilization also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:28.120
still endures. Though much of Breath of the Wild’s&nbsp;
world has been destroyed, there are pockets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:34.200
survivors from which a community could remerge.&nbsp;
Maybe that society would revere Levias — not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:41.840
for the god of the skies he once was, but for the&nbsp;
modest shelter his remains now offer. Zelda games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:47.240
have always had a sense of decaying divinity,&nbsp;
that the land itself was once something sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:52.600
and is now decomposing. Even the brightest&nbsp;
titles can capture this feeling. But there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:58.480
also a consistent sense that not all is lost yet,&nbsp;
that the world can be rebuilt on the bones of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:04.040
came before. “The giant is still alive for me,”&nbsp;
the narrator states at the end of The Drowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:09.640
Giant. “I often dream of his resurrection.&nbsp;
A colossus, striding through the streets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:17.520
town… picking up the fragments of himself on his&nbsp;
return journey to the sea…” And as always, thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:23.400
for watching. If you enjoyed this entry, please&nbsp;
lend your support by liking, subscribing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:49.360
hitting the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

